    "result_text": "Bulk open works: each claim has a checkbox, there's an \"Open selected\" button, and the screen shows how many claims were opened and how many were refused. `bin/e2e` passes.\n\n**One conflict to know about.** The request says claims are numbered \"newest claim first within a person\", but the test expects the opposite. In the test, Ada's older claim (10.00) gets number 2 and her newer one (20.00) gets number 3, and the audit file records them in that order. I followed the test, so the numbering goes person by person in the order people were added, then each person's claims oldest first. If newest first is really what you want, the test would need to change.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Server:** a new endpoint, `POST /api/sheets/{sheetId}/bulk-open` (`bulk-open.POST.yml`), runs in one transaction. A selected claim is opened if it's a draft and isn't dated inside a closed period, which is the same rule as opening a single claim. Each one opened gets the sheet's next number and a `CLAIM_OPENED` line in the audit file, in the same format as a single open. Every other selected claim is left as it is and counted as refused, and so is any id that isn't on the sheet. It also re-checks each claim's stored status after taking a number, as the single open does, so if two people open the same claims at the same moment, a claim can't be opened twice.\n- **Front end:**\n  - Each claim row has a `claim-select` checkbox. The ticked claims are shared across all the people's claim lists.\n  - A new bar, `bulkopen.slot.tsx`, holds the `bulk-open` button and shows the counts in `bulk-opened` and `bulk-refused`.\n  - After a successful run the ticks are cleared. The counts stay until you tick another claim or press the button again.\n  - If the whole request fails, it shows `bulk-error`.\n- No database change was needed.",
